1
00:00:00,350 --> 00:00:02,105
Lesson 4.10.

2
00:00:02,485 --> 00:00:06,149
Ising optimization and
invertible probabilistic logic.

3
00:00:07,250 --> 00:00:12,006
An Ising model at low temperature
concentrates on its minimum-energy states.

4
00:00:12,386 --> 00:00:17,180
If we manage to write a problem so
that its solution is the minimum of an

5
00:00:17,180 --> 00:00:21,317
Ising energy, we can solve it by
letting a pbit chip cool down.

6
00:00:21,697 --> 00:00:25,426
Many combinatorial optimization
problems, like maximum

7
00:00:25,426 --> 00:00:28,602
cut or satisfiability,
admit that formulation.

8
00:00:28,982 --> 00:00:32,003
And there is something
more surprising: logic

9
00:00:32,003 --> 00:00:35,092
circuits written this
way can be run backward.

10
00:00:36,217 --> 00:00:40,896
The maximum-cut problem asks to split
the nodes of a graph into two groups so

11
00:00:40,896 --> 00:00:44,846
that as many edges as possible
cross from one group to the other.

12
00:00:45,226 --> 00:00:47,768
Let's assign a spin to each node: plus one

13
00:00:47,768 --> 00:00:50,311
for one group and minus one for the other.

14
00:00:50,691 --> 00:00:55,031
An edge is cut if its
endpoints have opposite

15
00:00:55,031 --> 00:00:59,276
spins, and the cut
equals Σ (1 − s_i s_j)/2.

16
00:00:59,655 --> 00:01:03,857
Maximizing the cut is
minimizing an Ising energy

17
00:01:03,857 --> 00:01:07,447
with antiferromagnetic couplings, J = −w.

18
00:01:08,550 --> 00:01:12,254
Let's take a random graph with
ten nodes and eighteen edges.

19
00:01:12,634 --> 00:01:15,897
By brute force we know the
maximum cut is fourteen.

20
00:01:16,277 --> 00:01:19,800
With pbits and a three-hundred-sweep
anneal, the network

21
00:01:19,800 --> 00:01:23,135
finds a cut of fourteen in
all hundred runs we tried.

22
00:01:23,515 --> 00:01:26,734
For large graphs there are
no guarantees, but pbits

23
00:01:26,734 --> 00:01:30,142
explore many good solutions
in parallel and very fast.

24
00:01:31,250 --> 00:01:32,766
Now a logic circuit.

25
00:01:33,146 --> 00:01:37,726
For the AND gate, with variables zero or

26
00:01:37,726 --> 00:01:42,307
one, the penalty P = uv − 2uz − 2vz + 3z

27
00:01:42,687 --> 00:01:45,924
is exactly zero on the
four rows of the truth

28
00:01:45,924 --> 00:01:48,658
table, and positive on all the others.

29
00:01:49,038 --> 00:01:53,037
At β = 2, the four valid
rows each appear with

30
00:01:53,037 --> 00:01:57,732
probability 0.23: the network
samples the truth table.

31
00:01:58,112 --> 00:02:02,030
Any circuit is built by adding
the penalties of its gates.

32
00:02:03,150 --> 00:02:04,285
Here is the magic.

33
00:02:04,665 --> 00:02:08,522
Let's clamp the output z =
1 and leave the inputs free.

34
00:02:08,902 --> 00:02:15,063
The network finds the only compatible
input, one and one, with probability 0.79.

35
00:02:15,443 --> 00:02:17,604
If we clamp z = 0, it spreads the

36
00:02:17,604 --> 00:02:20,812
probability over the three
inputs that give zero.

37
00:02:21,192 --> 00:02:25,034
It is invertible logic: the same
circuit computes forward, by

38
00:02:25,034 --> 00:02:28,750
clamping the inputs, and
backward, by clamping the outputs.

39
00:02:29,850 --> 00:02:33,991
If we invert a multiplier,
clamping its output to a number N,

40
00:02:33,991 --> 00:02:37,588
the inputs search for two
factors whose product is N.

41
00:02:37,968 --> 00:02:40,847
It is factoring without
a factoring algorithm.

42
00:02:41,227 --> 00:02:46,836
With a toy model for N = 143, with
odd four-bit factors, annealing

43
00:02:46,836 --> 00:02:52,276
finds eleven times thirteen in
seventy-nine of two hundred runs.

44
00:02:52,656 --> 00:02:55,078
Careful: this does not break cryptography.

45
00:02:55,458 --> 00:02:59,318
The energy landscape of
factoring is very rugged, and the

46
00:02:59,318 --> 00:03:02,704
search time grows rapidly
with the number of bits.

47
00:03:03,817 --> 00:03:07,505
Any QUBO problem, of binary variables with

48
00:03:07,505 --> 00:03:11,808
quadratic cost, becomes
Ising with x = (1 + s)/2.

49
00:03:12,188 --> 00:03:15,484
Satisfiability uses
one penalty per clause.

50
00:03:15,864 --> 00:03:20,962
P-computers on FPGA already perform
billions of flips per second.

51
00:03:21,342 --> 00:03:23,366
And in unit seven we will see a

52
00:03:23,366 --> 00:03:27,023
factoring-by-diffusion algorithm
with proven guarantees.

53
00:03:28,150 --> 00:03:34,279
Common mistakes: penalties that are too
weak, which make invalid states cheaper.

54
00:03:34,659 --> 00:03:37,658
Forgetting the constant when
going from QUBO to Ising.

55
00:03:38,038 --> 00:03:42,334
Expecting polynomial-time
solutions of NP-hard problems.

56
00:03:42,714 --> 00:03:45,353
And forgetting that
higher-order terms need

57
00:03:45,353 --> 00:03:47,623
auxiliary spins on pairwise hardware.

58
00:03:48,750 --> 00:03:52,558
In summary: many optimization
problems are minima of Ising

59
00:03:52,558 --> 00:03:55,973
energies, and pbits search
for them by cooling down.

60
00:03:56,353 --> 00:04:00,802
Logic gates are written as
penalties whose minima are the truth

61
00:04:00,802 --> 00:04:04,828
table, and by clamping the
outputs circuits run backward.

62
00:04:05,208 --> 00:04:07,802
It is a powerful tool,
but it does not remove

63
00:04:07,802 --> 00:04:10,166
the intrinsic difficulty of the problems.

64
00:04:11,283 --> 00:04:14,332
To practice: check the AND penalty.

65
00:04:14,712 --> 00:04:17,757
Write the Ising model for the
maximum cut on a triangle.

66
00:04:18,137 --> 00:04:20,538
Convert the penalty to spins.

67
00:04:20,918 --> 00:04:23,299
And explain why the
clamped output does not

68
00:04:23,299 --> 00:04:25,347
give certainty at finite temperature.
